  "_about": [
    "MAJOR PORTS — the ports drawn first, biggest and boldest, from the furthest zoom (Stuart, 2026-11-02: 'the port of Felixstowe seems to be missing, isnt that one of the largest ports in Europe?').",
    "WHY A LIST: the NGA World Port Index (Pub. 150, public domain) grades HARBOUR size, not traffic — it calls Felixstowe, the UK's largest container port, 'Very Small', and Algeciras, Laem Chabang and Ningbo 'Small'. So a port in this list is class 0 (major) whatever its WPI grade; every other port keeps its WPI grade (L 1 · M 2 · S 3 · V 4).",
    "WHAT IS IN IT: the world's busiest container ports (public annual TEU league tables, e.g. the World Shipping Council's 'Top 50 World Container Ports'), plus the main cargo and ferry ports of the NAVTEX / AIS heartland (UK, Ireland, the North Sea, the Channel, the Baltic and the Mediterranean). Facts, not anyone's table copied: a port is either one of these or it is not.",
    "FIELDS: wpi = the WPI 'Main Port Name' exactly, cc = its 'Country Code' column exactly; label = the name to draw when the WPI's is not the one a reader knows (Antwerpen -> Antwerp). A port the WPI does not have (newer than its edition) gives lon/lat instead of wpi, and is added.",
    "Read by scripts/gen-map-data.mjs buildPorts (the JSON pack, Leaflet) and so by gen-map-tiles.mjs (the GL pack) — ONE list, two renderers."
  ],
